{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00352 - MONDO_grouped_18874_4643",
  "verbalization": "Tioguanine, a purine analog, is implicated in the treatment of myeloid leukemia through a mechanism involving its interaction with mitoxantrone, a chemotherapy agent. The evidence suggests that Tioguanine may be repurposed for the treatment of myeloid leukemia, as it is associated with indications for inherited and unclassified forms of acute myeloid leukemia, all of which are linked to mitoxantrone. This implies a potential pathway where Tioguanine could exert a therapeutic effect by influencing the same biological processes as mitoxantrone, possibly through shared molecular targets or pathways. The presence of multiple indirect pathways connecting Tioguanine to myeloid leukemia, all mediated through mitoxantrone, supports the hypothesis that Tioguanine may have a role in the treatment of this disease. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of mitoxantrone as a bridge between Tioguanine and myeloid leukemia, though further mechanistic validation is required to confirm the direct relationship.",
